- Keep the design in sheets and build from them, to keep the work small and checkable. Make JSON sheets, grouping like things together (items, weapons, enemies, systems, hooks into the game): each row is one thing and each column one of its properties, so a gun's row has damage, clip size and so on. Every game system the mashup touches is in a sheet. Each row generates one piece of code (a struct or its equivalent), built from that row's columns.
- The sheets are the source of truth. When unsure about anything, go back to them, and change the sheet before the code.
- Treat every row and column crossing as a checkbox. A row with an unfilled or unverified cell is unfinished; all of them must be checked.
- Preflight before every build: lay all the sheets' rows and columns over each other and list every unfilled cell and every reference between sheets that does not resolve. That shows what will fail or is unimplemented. Fix those first, and build only when the preflight is clean.
